      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>Nobody writes a sharper survey from memory. A report finished on site, with the risk in front of you, beats one rebuilt from notes two days later. So ask which tool makes finishing on site the easy choice.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>Insurance survey work covers risk surveys, loss adjusting, property risk and fire risk. Each has its own report shape.</li><li>The best report is made on site, not from memory.</li><li>General inspection apps and dictation tools work, but add an install, a login and seat costs.</li><li>A WhatsApp workflow needs no install. You already use the app.</li><li>Software makes a clean record. It does not make the survey valid. You do, with RICS or PAS 79 standards where they apply.</li><li>£75 per month per phone number & unlimited free dashboard accounts.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The landscape",
      "heading": "What counts as an insurance survey",
      "html": "<p>A commercial insurance risk survey records construction, occupancy, protection and exposure (COPE) so the insurer can price it. A loss adjuster's site report follows a claim. It sets scope, cause and reinstatement cost. A property risk assessment looks at perils such as fire, flood, escape of water and security. A fire risk assessment under the Regulatory Reform (Fire Safety) Order 2005 is its own regulated job, often to PAS 79.</p><p>Each has its own report and reader. They share one weakness: the gap between the visit and the write-up. Detail slips with every hour. Record on site and you close the gap.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Category one",
      "heading": "General inspection and audit apps",
      "html": "<p>Tools such as iAuditor (SafetyCulture) are built for anyone who inspects anything. You can build a survey template in them. The catch is setup and training. Prices are per seat. Office staff who only read reports cost the same as your busiest surveyor.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Category two",
      "heading": "Dictation and document tools",
      "html": "<p>Many surveyors dictate into a Word template and tidy it up later. It is quick to start. But the tidying happens off site. The structure lives in your head. Two surveyors make two different reports. The office cannot see progress.</p><p>It suits a sole surveyor with a good routine. It does not scale. There is no dashboard and no set list of questions. The section skipped on a wet Tuesday is the one nobody sees is missing.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Category three",
      "heading": "Conversation-based workflows",
      "html": "<p>The newer way has no new app. The survey arrives as a WhatsApp chat. Each question is a message. You reply by text, voice note or photo. Then the report is made, with a web link, a Word or PDF copy and a permanent Record. There is nothing to install and very little to learn.</p><p>Quickler works this way. Photos, voice notes and typed answers go to the right place. The office sees each survey's status on a dashboard.</p>"
    },
